          "question": "I am 52 and my LDL cholesterol is 165 mg/dL. Does that number alone decide treatment, what risk bands does guidance use, and which modifiable coronary-risk factors does the corpus describe?",
          "answer": "**Short answer: no.** Guidance does not decide treatment from LDL alone. It uses your estimated 10-year risk of heart and blood-vessel disease, which combines several measures (*Lipids: screening intervals, risk stratification and statin initiation*). Your age, 52, is in the range where that estimate applies. [removed: this system does not give doses or amounts.]\n\n**Risk bands: the sources disagree on what the corpus contains.** One review of the retrieved material could not find the band thresholds or the 10-year treatment cut-off, so it cannot tell you where your value falls. A second review reported bands from the lipid guidance: low below 5%, borderline 5% to below 7.5%, intermediate 7.5% to below 20%, and high 20% or above. Because the first review's factual claims take precedence, I cannot confirm those bands from the retrieved passages. Your clinician can tell you which band applies to you.\n\n[removed: this system does not give doses or amounts.] Your value is below that line.\n- Risk-enhancing features can shift decisions in the borderline and intermediate bands. These include family history of early heart disease, chronic kidney disease, chronic inflammatory conditions, persistently raised triglycerides, and metabolic syndrome. A coronary artery calcium score may help where the decision is unclear.\n[removed: this system does not give doses or amounts.]\n- US and European guidance frame LDL targets differently: US guidance describes statin intensity and percentage reduction, while European guidance sets explicit LDL goals that fall as risk rises. The sources say these should not be mixed, and they do not give the numeric targets.\n- The sources do not grade how strong the evidence is for these thresholds, so ask your clinician about the evidence behind their recommendation.\n\n**Modifiable coronary risk factors in the corpus** (*Coronary artery disease*): smoking, high blood pressure, raised LDL cholesterol, diabetes, obesity, physical inactivity, a diet high in saturated fat and low in vegetables and fibre, chronic kidney disease, and inflammatory conditions. Age, male sex and family history of early heart disease are also listed as contributors. One source says family history cannot be changed, so its wording on which factors are modifiable is unclear. The sources do not explain how each factor affects LDL or how much each adds to risk.\n\n**General lifestyle measures** (*Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line*): less dietary salt, more vegetables, fruit and whole grains, low-fat dairy, less saturated fat, regular aerobic activity, a healthy weight, limited alcohol, and not smoking. The guidance says these effects are partly additive. It is about blood pressure, not LDL specifically.\n\n[removed: this system does not give doses or amounts.]\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
